// A recipe, written from md-docs/recipes/serve-with-nginx.md by scripts/docsite/recipes.py;
// edit the recipe, not this file.
page RecipeServeWithNginx(path: "/recipes/serve-with-nginx", title: "Serve a site with nginx — Recipes", description: "Serve a WebFluent build with nginx: a server block with the build's gzip files, a 404 page, a single-page fallback, cache and security headers.") {
    RecipeShell("Serve a site with nginx", summary: "This recipe serves a WebFluent build from a server of your own with nginx: the precompressed files the build wrote, its 404 page, and the cache and security headers.  You need a server running nginx with a TLS certificate for the site, and a `build/` folder from `wf build` ([Install wf on a build machine](/recipes/install-wf-in-ci)).  Put the security headers in a file of their own, so every block that needs them can include them:", outputs: ["website", "app"], since: "", recipes: RECIPE_INDEX, sections: [Anchor(id: "how-it-works", label: "How it works"), Anchor(id: "check-it", label: "Check it")], related: [RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Serve a site with Apache", to: "/recipes/serve-with-apache"), RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Install wf on a build machine", to: "/recipes/install-wf-in-ci"), RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Set cache and security headers", to: "/recipes/cache-and-security-headers")], source: "md-docs/recipes/serve-with-nginx.md", how: true, hasVariations: false, project: {}) {
        code {
            CodeBlock(#"# /etc/nginx/snippets/site-headers.conf
add_header X-Content-Type-Options nosniff always;
add_header Referrer-Policy strict-origin-when-cross-origin always;
add_header X-Frame-Options DENY always;
# add_header Content-Security-Policy "…as in build/_headers…" always;"#, title: "nginx")
            Markdown("Then add this `server` block, and pick the `try_files` line for your kind of build:")
            CodeBlock(#"server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    server_name example.com;
    ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/privkey.pem;
    root /var/www/site;

    gzip_static on;                          # serve the .gz files the build wrote
    include snippets/site-headers.conf;

    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ $uri.html =404; # a static build
        # try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;  # a single-page build
    }
    error_page 404 /404.html;

    location = /sw.js {
        include snippets/site-headers.conf;
        add_header Cache-Control "no-cache";
    }

    # Pictures the build resized and renamed: hero.4a6178a1.960.webp, hero.4a6178a1.jpg
    location ~* "\.[0-9a-f]{8}(\.[0-9]+)?\.(webp|avif|jpe?g|png|gif)$" {
        include snippets/site-headers.conf;
        add_header Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable";
    }
}"#, title: "nginx")
            Markdown("Upload the build with any tool — for example, `rsync` — and reload nginx:")
            Terminal(lines: [
                        Line(command: "rsync -a --delete build/ server:/var/www/site/")
                    ])
        }
        Markdown("1. `root` is the folder the build is uploaded to.\n2. `gzip_static on` sends the `.gz` file the build wrote beside a text file when the browser accepts gzip, so nginx compresses nothing itself.\n3. `try_files` decides what a path reaches. In a static build, `/contact` is `contact/index.html` (`$uri/`) or, with `clean_urls: \"file\"`, `contact.html` (`$uri.html`); anything else is a 404. In a single-page build, every path falls back to `index.html`, where the router draws the page.\n4. `error_page 404 /404.html` serves the page whose `path` is `*`.\n5. `sw.js` is never cached without asking. The pictures the build resized and renamed (`img/hero.<hash>.<width>.webp`, and the original as `img/hero.<hash>.jpg`) are cached for a year, since a changed picture gets a new name. A file of `public/` keeps the name you gave it, so it is left to the browser's usual checks: a new `/logo.png` reaches readers at once ([Set cache headers](/recipes/cache-and-security-headers#set-cache-headers)).\n6. The `add_header` lines are the security headers the build writes to `build/_headers`. Copy the `Content-Security-Policy` line from that file into the commented line to send the policy as a header too.\n7. nginx drops the server's `add_header` lines in any `location` that has an `add_header` of its own. That is why the two locations that set `Cache-Control` include the headers file again: without it, `sw.js` and the pictures would be sent with no security headers.\n8. `ssl_certificate` and `ssl_certificate_key` name the certificate the `listen 443 ssl` line needs; the paths above are where Certbot puts them.\n\n`rsync -a --delete` makes the server's folder match `build/`, removing files an older build left behind.")

        Heading("Check it", id: "check-it").h2

        Markdown("`curl -I` a page and check the headers. Ask for a compressed copy and check that nginx sends the build's `.gz` file:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "curl -sI -H \"Accept-Encoding: gzip\" https://example.com/app.js")
            ])

        Markdown("The response has `Content-Encoding: gzip`. Then go through [Check a deploy](/recipes/check-a-deploy).")

    }
}
